{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfiGQ3bwTfsceieu6ofs5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the round-0 candidate for slot 5 (`person_specific_chance_switch_belief`), and it passed the self-check. That check is only a short smoke fit, so it says nothing yet about convergence, reliable model comparison or novelty against the other models. Admission's full fit will decide those.\n\n**Model refined:** `bayesian_chance_vs_motif_beta_trick_coin`, ranked 1 and statistically tied with the incumbent. People judge a sequence random to the extent their idea of a fair coin explains it better than three \"regular\" explanations: a coin that tends to switch or repeat, a biased coin, and a short pattern that repeats with occasional slips.\n\n**The one change:** how often a person thinks a fair coin switches sides is now set per participant, drawn from a population distribution. Before, everyone shared one value. A person can now believe a fair coin tends to repeat, and for them streakier sequences look more random. No live model has a per-person belief like this; the others share it and vary only in how decisively people choose. Spare person slots let the model predict new participants, so the next experiment's design can use it.\n\n**Why this change:** it targets the one critique of the current best model that held up after correcting for multiple tests. 18.8% of participants chose the sequence with fewer switches more than half the time, against 5.0% predicted (q = 0.016). The best model can't produce that, because everyone shares the same direction of preference.\n\n**Self-check result:** it printed `OK` after a 100-draw, single-chain fit, with a finite log-probability and the data contract met.\n- **Score:** the smoke-fit ELPD-LOO was −2122.7, against −2318.3 for the current best model. That would be a large gain, but it comes from a short fit, so treat it as a hint until the full fit confirms it.\